course: dictionary
lesson: 3667
title: Old words, fitting places
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words come from old books and sound a bit grand. «Fitly» is one of them. You will meet it in stories from long ago and in the Bible. The words around it will give you the clues.
explain: Look at «where heroes could fitly feast after victory, where weary harvesters could line up in scores along the table». Heroes, feast and victory all belong to a happy celebration. So fitly must describe a feast that is just right for the heroes. Try the swap: «where heroes could rightly feast after victory» still makes sense, so fitly means rightly.
explain: Modern English would say «properly» or «aptly». Use the same test every time: swap in a simple word and check the sentence still makes sense. In your own writing, use properly or rightly unless you want an old-fashioned feel.
fact: Fitly is an adverb, so it tells us how something is done.
fact: «Fitly» means in a suitable or proper way; rightly.
fact: «Fitly» is formal and old-fashioned, so you will mostly find it in old books and the Bible.
fact: In the Bible, Proverbs 25:11 says «a word fitly spoken is like apples of gold», meaning a word said in the right way at the right time.
fact: Modern English would say «properly» or «aptly» instead of fitly.
exercise: «The children were fitly dressed for the wedding.» What does fitly mean here? | Without any clothes at all | *In a suitable, proper way | Quickly and carelessly | Very loudly
exercise: «A word fitly spoken» is an old saying. Which modern word could replace fitly? | loudly | sadly | secretly | *properly
exercise: Which words in «where heroes could fitly feast after victory» are the clues to its meaning? | where and could | fitly and after | *heroes, feast and victory | table and harvesters
test: Mum says the speech was fitly delivered. What does she mean? | It was far too short | *It was given in a suitable, proper way | It was delivered by a very fit athlete | It was late and rude
test: Which word means the opposite of fitly? | properly | rightly | aptly | *unsuitably
exam: Chloe says fitly means fit and healthy. Is she right? | Yes, it means healthy and strong | No, it means very loud | Yes, it means very fast | *No, it means in a suitable or proper way
